Keith Davies wrote:
> Derek Ray <moot@idont_check_the_spamtrap_anyway.com> wrote:
>> If he has a cursed Excalibur, he may have gotten it from a bonesfile
>> and thus not know the +X status.  If he doesn't know the +X status,
>> it's dangerous to read blessed enchant weapon.
> 
> How often do you run into a heavily-enhanted Excalibur in a bones file?

More frequently than you'd think?  Excalibur is one of the easiest
"good" artifacts to obtain; Knights and Valkyries start with a
longsword, and any other lawful character only needs to pick one up and
hit level 5.

> I'll admit, I haven't played as many games as many of the people here,
> but ISTM that if someone does get a heavily-enchanted Excalibur he's
> somewhat unlikely to die where someone this early in the game (and this
> early in his nethack career) would find him.

It's far from unusual to get caught by instakills on the way back up
from Sokoban going to Minetown, _or_ leaving Minetown.  As far as it
being heavily enchanted, someone in either case could easily have (and
have used) several enchant weapon scrolls; and the Valkyrie's longsword
starts at +1.

It is a fairly high dungeon level.  But people die all over the place;
it's amazing what kind of bones files there are available to find.

> Unless the first guy got lucky with a bunch of ?oEW, and the second guy
> got lucky enough to find the bones, of course.  It's not impossible,
> after all, and if it's not impossible it happens in this game.

There are any number of scenarios imaginable; it's best to remember that
nothing is impossible, and to take nothing for granted.

I've seen someone playing on NAO zap the castle wand once, wish for
charging, and then zap it again only to see "You wrest a final charge
from the worn-out wand."  Oops.  Very, very unlikely, of course, and
I'll freely admit I only know of it happening once (has to be found 0:1,
have to hit the what, 1/121 chance? on the first try, and have to have
NOT identified the wand arbitrarily)... but still, the chance is there.
 Does that mean you should never zap the wand a second time?  No, but if
you do have any identify scrolls available, it costs you nothing to
waste one and make sure.  (Or if you know the spell, of course.)

> So, it's *probably* -- almost certainly, I think -- safe to use ?oEW in
> this case.

A big part of this game is minimizing risk.  The difference between 11
and 10 holy waters is negligible at worst.  Put simply, it costs you
nothing to just #dip from this situation, and it could cost you your
good artifact weapon to read ?oEW.

If you only had 1 holy water, things might be quite different, and I
might be inclined to take the risk in that case.  But with 11?  #Dip.

